From: Thorch contributors
Subject: [PATCH] input: qcom-hv-haptics: re-arm the per-play stop timer

0226 arms stop_work from haptics_playback() with
schedule_delayed_work(). That does not re-arm a work item that is
already pending: it returns false and leaves the existing deadline in
place. A tap that arrives while the previous play's stop is still
pending therefore inherits the previous deadline instead of getting its
own, so its buzz is truncated to whatever time was left.

With a short replay length the truncation is severe and repeats
predictably. At 40 ms on the AYN Thor's on-screen keyboard, a tap 35 ms
after the previous one gets 5 ms of motor, the following tap gets a full
40 ms because the timer has fired and cleared, and the pattern alternates
strong/weak for as long as taps keep landing inside the pending window.
Longer replay lengths hid it only because taps rarely landed that close.

Use mod_delayed_work(), which always re-arms to the new deadline. Each
play now owns its stop regardless of what came before it. There is no
kernel-side replay timer to race with here: this driver registers
through input_ff_create() rather than the ff-memless path, so ff-core
never re-arms or expires the effect on the client's behalf, and
stop_work is the only deadline governing a constant play.

system_percpu_wq has to be passed explicitly. Unlike
schedule_delayed_work(), mod_delayed_work() has no implicit default
queue, and this work is bound with INIT_DELAYED_WORK() at probe, which
does not bind one either. system_percpu_wq is what
schedule_delayed_work() resolves to, so the work stays on the queue the
rest of the driver already uses.

The two schedule_delayed_work() calls in the FF_CONSTANT upload gain
path are left alone: they are preceded by cancel_delayed_work_sync(), so
the work cannot already be pending there.

Fixes: 0226-input-qcom-haptics-arm-stop-per-ff-constant-play.patch
---
 drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
@@ -2881,9 +2881,20 @@
 		 * every play is bounded by the effect's own replay length.
 		 * Without this a client that uploads one effect and plays it
 		 * many times runs the motor until something else stops it.
+		 *
+		 * mod_delayed_work(), not schedule_delayed_work(): the latter
+		 * leaves an already pending timer at its old deadline instead
+		 * of re-arming it, so a tap that arrives before the previous
+		 * stop fires inherits that stop's deadline and is cut short.
+		 *
+		 * system_percpu_wq is passed explicitly because
+		 * INIT_DELAYED_WORK() binds no workqueue and
+		 * mod_delayed_work() has no implicit default. It is the queue
+		 * schedule_delayed_work() resolves to, so this keeps the work
+		 * on the queue the rest of the driver already uses.
 		 */
 		if (chip->constant_replay_ms > 0)
-			schedule_delayed_work(&chip->stop_work,
+			mod_delayed_work(system_percpu_wq, &chip->stop_work,
 					msecs_to_jiffies(chip->constant_replay_ms));
 
 		return 0;
